Subject: [PATCH 05717/11362] [outer-loop] research note: staged plan for
 TRM-as-generator on hidden games

Operator asked, granting that public-game TRM training succeeds: "what is
the plan for a TRM based generator that may solve the hidden game levels
in the live agent?" Answers the natural follow-on to the TRM-as-generator
note and the human-replay-corpus finding, both from earlier today.

The core framing: a TRM trained on the 25 public games' mechanics does
not directly solve a hidden game, because the hidden game's specific
rules are by definition something the model never saw -- this project's
own settled framing (feedback_arc_value_is_process_not_weights). Every
stage of the plan is built around what generalizes (a recipe, an
adaptation mechanism), never around the Stage 1 checkpoint being the
hidden-game solver itself.

4-stage plan, each gated on the one before:
1. Public-game training produces a validated recipe + general prior, not
   a solver -- gated on the leave-one-game-out pilot showing genuine
   held-out generalization, not memorization.
2. Wires in as a generator feeding the EXISTING WorldModelVerifier gate in
   E3AgentPolicy -- additive only, per "ARC Live-Path Reachability
   Discipline," never a bypass.
3. The load-bearing stage: online, within-episode full-fine-tune
   adaptation of the TRM checkpoint on the hidden game's own accumulated
   transitions as the agent plays -- mirrors the existing arc_live_ttt
   online world-model induction pattern already used by the scored agent
   (full-FT not LoRA, per this project's own TRM test-time-adaptation
   finding). Flagged the adaptation cadence as its own open, unmeasured
   question rather than assumed.
4. Mandatory guardrail: TRM must never regress the baseline solve rate --
   reuses the solve_rate_dropped pattern already specified in exp4490's
   own capability spec.

docs/research-notes/trm-generator-hidden-game-plan-2026-07-04.md - full
writeup. Cross-linked from the original TRM note. Flagged in
ops/known-issues.md for planner pickup, explicit that skipping straight
from Stage 1 to live deployment without Stages 2-4 would repeat the exact
"weights don't transfer" mistake this project already learned from once.

